<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.5.3: Using Correlation Coefficient to Describe Relationships between Two Variables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00779</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.5.3: Using Correlation Coefficient to Describe Relationships between Two Variables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>13ba9c14-7bfa-46f0-a5f8-8e89983cfd4a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>For each situation, describe the linear relationship between the variables, based on the correlation coefficient. Make sure to mention whether there is a <emphasis>strong relationship</emphasis> or not as well as whether it is a <emphasis>positive relationship</emphasis> or <emphasis>negative relationship</emphasis>.</para>
<!--Question 1 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
<para>Number of steps taken per day and number of kilometers walked per day. \(r = 0.92\)
    </para>
</problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>There is a strong, positive linear relationship. An increase in the number of kilometers walked tends to go along with an increase in the number of steps taken per day.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 2 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
<para>Temperature of a rubber band and distance the rubber band can stretch. \(r = 0.84\)
    </para>
<para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  </problem><solution>
  
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>There is a strong, positive linear relationship. An increase in temperature tends to go along with an increase in the distance a rubber band can stretch.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 3 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
<para>Car weight and distance traveled using a full tank of gas. \(r = - 0.86\)
    </para>
<para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  </problem><solution>
  
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>There is a strong, negative relationship. An increase in the weight of a car tends to go along with a decrease in the distance it can travel on a full tank of gas.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 4 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
<para>Average fat intake per citizen of a country and average cancer rate of a country. \(r = 0.73\)
    </para>
<para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  </problem><solution>
  
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>There is a moderate positive linear relationship. An increase in the fat intake per citizen of a country tends to go along with an increase in the average cancer rate of a country, but it is not a strong relationship.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 5 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
 <para>Score on science exam and number of words written on the essay question. \(r = 0.28\)
     </para>
<para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  </problem><solution>
  
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>There is a weak, positive linear relationship. An increase in the number of words written on the essay question tends to go along with an increase on the score for a science exam, but the relationship is weak.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 6 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
<para>Average time spent listening to music per day and average time spent watching TV per day. \(r = - 0.17\)
    </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  </problem><solution>
  
    <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>There is a weak, negative relationship. An increase in the average time spent listening to music per day tends to go along with a decrease in the average time spent watching TV per day, although the relationship is weak.</para>
  </solution></exercise>


